tened to well I disagree  with Russell I think I think the trouble  is politics is very complex that is  unfortunately the situation it is that  complex and we need to get it and  simplify it I think the extremist  parties come up with a very simplistic  politics which I disagree with but real  politics center right center left it is  actually very complex but you  need to inspire the younger generation  you need to inspire young people bring  the voting age down to 1617 you know  this is this is something would be more  positive and I think the something if  you take like the Olympic Games which  isn't a vertical itical but young people  involved in that people of all ages were  involved in that I was very keen and  happy to be involved in it and that was  great to see everyone working together  with a mission people need a mission and  if you can have a vision of the future  that actually goes somewhere that's  positive I think that will appeal more  to young people Eddy I'm just going to  put a couple of your points to our  panelists and others here and adult and  Eddie was talking there about lowering  the voting age to 16 or 17 we've seen  that in Scotland in the Scottish  referendum there's been a lot of  engagement in that age group do you  think that would work here I think it's  a great idea yes you should lower it for  1617 improve citizenship classes make  perhaps politics more engaging and  perhaps a compulsory in schools and  inform them as to how they can help in  their local area as well as the regional  and the wider area in the UK politics in  schools is something that's come up  repeatedly throughout the day and  through our contributors that on the  stand up be counted up as well that  people aren't being taught politics in  schools so how are you expected to know  who to vote for when you turn 18 well  you just don't and you're so  disillusioned by it by the time you  reach the age that you are actually able  to make difference and vote that you  just have no idea about what you should  vote for but the thing is young people  are more connected than ever so why  don't we tap into that and so  politicians should ideally link in to  that and try and get their voice try and  get young people's voices heard by the  politicians yeah let's just put that to  Eddie Izzard young people are tapped in  the technology is there politicians need  to do more to engage in a social media  sense absolutely they do I should add to  this the way figures were coming up to  this election if people feel who are  young people they want to be involved  become activists you can get out there  you could be on the streets you could be  meeting people who are activists and  trying to make a change in whichever  party you join so there is the  opportunity there in coming up to next  May's general election for young people  to  get out but sometimes it's really  difficult to choose exactly what you  feel and I didn't join the political  parties till I was 35 but yes social  media absolutely it engages young people  and then you have to put this vision on  top you have to have a vision of the  future that it's going to inspire people  you're talking to us from the United  States President Obama of course had a  lot of young people involved in his  presidential campaign how did he manage  that and how what difference did it make  well he was a black man running to be  the first black president in the United  States of America and that really got  through to people it was a different  thing I think he's had great difficulty  then trying to put all the policies he'd  like into action because the extremists  on the right wing the Tea Party  particularly were just trying to stamp  down and make him not be able to do  anything but he has struggled hard to  push through agenda with his health care  which they disparagingly called Obama  care but now in the Democratic Party  they they say we love Obama care and  that's it's taken so long for that to  happen we got that in 1945 in Britain  and they've only just got that through  in the last year so here in America do  you think part of the problem though is  is that our politicians don't have the  right charisma charisma is a you can't  buy it from a shop it's a tricky old  thing and that goes hand in hand with  inspiration you tries to try and lead by  example I mean I you know I'm very  positive on Europe I believe Europe is  an adventure if you have the guts to go  for it I'm now touring France in French  touring Germany and German just started  Spanish then I want to do Russian there  is a way of doing things I came out as a  trans as being a transvestite when I was  23 and I've been open and honest about  that since then and so you've got to try  and come up with ideas which are game  changes which changed the way that  people live their lives in a positive  way I hope and that's what I've tried to  do and I think other politicians need to  do that as well honesty is a word there  Eddie Izzard was using that's one of the  words that's come up in all of our polls  that people value the most young people  particularly value the most in our  politicians what do you think of when  you think of today's politicians cancer  we do inevitably think of just the guys  at Westminster it's not varied enough we  need well I would argue that we did war  women in politics more young people  because that way the David Clemens  reshuffle do anything for you well that  is probably another whole whole debate  I'd say and that we just I think we just  need that the politicians need to reach  out through young people because they  make up such a large number of the  population that just currently isn't  really reached or targeted when you look  at Prime Minister's Questions and you  see the shouting that some would call it  the stuffiness others would describe it  does that engage young people it can  appear a fast sometimes but it is a bit  engaging you can see that they are human  on earth they are human like us they are  passionate in some sense that it is out  of hand a few times I've sat in PM cues  from the gallery and it is a bit of a  farce you do lose concentration you kind  of lose the meaning of the point that  they're there so it's a mixed bag really  and I think it does need a bit of  reining in the speaker does have to do a  better job okay let's just get a final  thought from Eddie Izzard here as we  start to move towards the 2015 election  Eddie Izzard what advice would you give  to Mr Cameron Mr Miliband Mr Clegg to  make sure that they do engage with young  voters inspire a generation that's what  they've tried to do in the Olympics and  I think they did it yes you've got to be  inspirational you've got to have a  vision and then you fight your corner I  suppose but yes it needs to appeal to  young people and I think vision is the  thing okay Eddie Izzard Cassie Galpin  and a tall resident thanks very much  indeed for your thoughts

